  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Cut off low takes residence over NorCal\nthis morning before ejecting east along the northern Nevada state\nline Friday morning. The position of the low over NorCal today \nand tomorrow will produce elevated south-southwestelry winds \nacross most of the Great Basin and especially across central \nNevada. The strongest winds will be focused across White Pine \nCounty and gusts 25 to 35 MPH, reaching 45 MPH across the high \nterrain should be expected. Patchy blowing dust will be present. \nThe upper low and associated weak surface boundary will not \nproduce significant rainfall with atmospheric moisture being the \nlimiting factor. However, that being said, locations along the \nIdaho and Oregon borders may experience a brief light rain shower \nor two. Isolated dry thunderstorms will also be possible though \nstorm coverage looks to be less than 15%. Concurrently, \nsignificant moisture across Utah, thanks to a northerly push of \nsubtropical moisture from the desert southwest, will spill into \nportions of eastern Nevada and provide a 10% to 20% chance for \nrain showers and dry thunderstorms along the Utah state line. The \neastern half of White Pine County, primarily over the Snake Range,\nwill again be the most likely location for any shower and \nthunderstorm activity as atmospheric condtions look most \nfavorable here. \n\nAs the cut off low ejects east along the Nevada border Friday \nmorning, moisture will also be pushed east out of the forecast \narea, producing dry weather and generally light winds for this \nweekend. Temperatures across northern Nevada will cool several \ndegrees Thursday night and Friday as the low and associated cooler\nair mass move east. \n\nTemperatures gradually warm thru the weekend with a return to\ndaytime highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s across the valleys by\nSunday. A low chance (10%) for rain showers across central Nevada\nwill be present early next week as a weak Pacific low pressure \nsystem moves into central California from the west. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence remains in\nelevated fire weather conditions today and Thursday. Moderate \nconfidence exists for showers and thunderstorms along the northern\nNV border and in eastern White Pine County the remainder of the \nweek. Significant grid edits were not necessary at this time \nthough did massage PoP grids within White Pine County to better \nrepresent thunderstorm chances.",
